{"step_index":88,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-30T13:58:21Z","input_tokens":2832,"cache_read_tokens":113080,"output_tokens":4849,"content":"# QUEUE ORACLE 🏥\n### AI-Powered Smart Hospital Queue & Resource Prediction System\n\n> **Vision:** *\"Queue Oracle does not just show what is happening in a hospital. It predicts what could happen next and helps hospitals prepare.\"*\n\nA complete, fully functional end-to-end prototype of **Queue Oracle** has been built and tested in your project workspace at:\n`C:\\Users\\KRUTIKA SHIVANAND\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\queue-oracle`\n\n---\n\n## 1. Project Directory Structure\n\n```\nqueue-oracle/\n│\n├── app.py                     # Main Flask REST API & Web Router\n├── database.py                # SQLite schema (8 relational tables) & connection helpers\n├── data_generator.py          # Synthetic dataset generator (>1500 records) & DB seeder\n├── train_model.py             # ML training (Random Forest & Linear), evaluation & saving\n├── prediction.py              # ML inference & dynamic queue delay recalculation engine\n├── disease_forecasting.py     # Aggregate epidemiological analysis & tomorrow's forecast\n├── resource_management.py     # Bed occupancy (+1h-4h forecast), inventory & staffing\n├── recommendations.py         # Doctor workload rebalancing & admin decision support\n├── forecasting.py             # Diurnal hourly arrivals & waiting time curve modeling\n├── test_app.py                # 10/10 automated end-to-end integration test suite\n├── requirements.txt           # Python dependencies\n├── README.md                  # Detailed documentation & hackathon jury guide\n│\n├── dataset/\n│   ├── synthetic_patients.csv # 1,500 realistic historical patient training records\n│   └── historical_disease.csv # 30-day surveillance history across 5 conditions\n│\n├── models/\n│   ├── wait_time_rf.joblib     # Saved Random Forest Regression model artifact\n│   ├── wait_time_linear.joblib # Saved Multiple Linear Regression baseline artifact\n│   └── model_metrics.json      # Actual validation metrics (MAE, RMSE, R², Feature Weights)\n│\n├── static/\n│   ├── css/\n│   │   └── style.css          # Professional medical dashboard styling (light blue/white, navy sidebar)\n│   └── js/\n│       ├── charts.js          # Chart.js visualization handlers for all 9 charts\n│       ├── dashboard.js       # Client router, table renderers & status dispatchers\n│       └── simulation.js      # Hackathon interactive simulation lab triggers\n│\n├── templates/\n│   └── index.html             # Single-page dashboard containing all 14 functional sections\n│\n└── hospital.db                # SQLite database with initialized clinical records\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Technology Stack & Environment\n\nAll required libraries are installed and verified:\n- **Backend:** Python 3.12, Flask 3.1.3, Flask-CORS 6.0.5\n- **Machine Learning:** Scikit-Learn 1.9.1 (Random Forest Regressor, Linear Regression, Moving Average Time-Series)\n- **Data Science:** Pandas 3.0.5, NumPy 2.5.4, Joblib 1.6.0\n- **Database:** SQLite3 (`hospital.db` with relational foreign keys)\n- **Frontend:** HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript (ES6 Fetch API), Bootstrap 5.3.3, Chart.js 4.4.2, FontAwesome 6.5.1\n\n---\n\n## 3. How to Run Queue Oracle\n\nOpen a terminal (PowerShell or Command Prompt) on your laptop and run:\n\n```powershell\n# 1. Navigate to the project directory\ncd \"C:\\Users\\KRUTIKA SHIVANAND\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\queue-oracle\"\n\n# 2. Run the application\npython app.py\n```\n\nNow open your web browser and go to:\n### **👉 `http://127.0.0.1:5000`**\n\n*(Note: To set this directory as your active workspace in Antigravity, click **File > Open Folder** and choose `C:\\Users\\KRUTIKA SHIVANAND\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\queue-oracle`.)*\n\n---\n\n## 4. Key Features Implemented\n\n### 🧠 1. AI-Based Patient Waiting Time Prediction\n- Trained on **1,500 patient records** using a **Random Forest Regressor** with input features: `patients_ahead`, `avg_consult_duration`, `priority_code` (Normal, Medium, Emergency), `doctor_workload`, `doctor_available`, `arrival_hour`, `dept_code`, and `emergency_interruptions`.\n- Enter any token number (e.g. **`101`** or **`143`**) in the **Wait Time Prediction** view to see:\n  - **OPD Token #** & **Assigned Doctor**\n  - **Patients Ahead** in live queue\n  - **Predicted Waiting Time** (e.g., *65 minutes*)\n  - **Expected Consultation Clock** (e.g., *12:05 PM*)\n  - Full **AI Explainability breakdown** of input weights.\n\n### ⏱️ 2. Dynamic Waiting Time Recalculation Engine\n- **Delay Cascading:** When a consultation runs longer than expected (e.g., +25 minutes), clicking **Record Delay** detects the discrepancy and **cascades the delay to all subsequent patients in that doctor's queue**, shifting expected consultation times forward.\n- **Early Completion:** If a doctor finishes early (-10 minutes), subsequent wait times decrease dynamically.\n- **Emergency Queue Jumping:** When an **Emergency** patient arrives, the engine reprioritizes the queue immediately, placing them first while adjusting all normal-priority patients' expected clocks.\n\n### ⚖️ 3. Intelligent Doctor Workload Balancing\n- The system continuously compares doctor queues in each department (e.g., **Dr. Rajesh Sharma has 16 patients** while **Dr. Priya Nair has only 3** in General Medicine).\n- It formulates a specific proposal: *\"Reassign 6 eligible Normal-priority patients from Dr. Rajesh Sharma to Dr. Priya Nair, reducing Dr. Sharma's queue wait by ~55 mins.\"*\n- Click **APPROVE REASSIGNMENT** or **REJECT REASSIGNMENT**:\n  - Upon approval, the patients' `assigned_doctor_id` is updated in SQLite.\n  - Queues and predicted wait times for both doctors recalculate instantly.\n\n### 🛏️ 4. Department-Wise Bed Occupancy & 4-Hour Hourly Forecast\n- Tracks total beds, occupied beds, and available beds across 6 wards (General Medicine, Pediatrics, Orthopedics, Cardiology, Emergency, General Surgery).\n- Uses OPD intake conversion rates and discharge schedules to project bed capacity for **+1 Hour, +2 Hours, +3 Hours, and +4 Hours**.\n- Features automated color thresholds: **GREEN (<75%)**, **ORANGE (75%–89%)**, **RED (≥90% / Shortage Alert)**.\n- For example, Cardiology triggers an alert: *\"Cardiology may experience bed capacity shortage within the next three hours.\"*\n\n### 🦟 5. Disease Trend Surveillance & Hospital Preparedness\n- Aggregate epidemiological surveillance for **Dengue, Malaria, Seasonal Influenza, and Fever-related illnesses** over the past 7 and 30 days.\n- Combines **Linear Regression** trends with **Moving Average** time-series to project tomorrow's demand.\n- **Direct Inventory Linkage:** Spikes in Dengue immediately check available stocks of **Dengue NS1 Antigen Rapid Test Kits** and **IV Normal Saline (0.9% 500ml)**, warning administrators of stockout risks before supplies run dry.\n- *Includes a clear disclaimer distinguishing aggregate surveillance from clinical diagnosis.*\n\n### 📦 6. Inventory & Staffing Decision Support\n- Monitors hospital supplies, daily burn rates, safety thresholds, and days until stockout.\n- One-click **Restock** capability to replenish supplies.\n- Analyzes patient-to-doctor ratios across departments to recommend temporary nurse/physician surge reallocations.\n- Consolidated **Administrator Insights** module with **Approve**, **Reject**, and **Mark Completed** audit history.\n\n### 🧪 7. Interactive Hackathon Demonstration Simulation Lab\nA dedicated playground specifically created to demonstrate dynamic recalculations to judges:\n1. **Burst Patient Arrivals:** Add **+5** or **+10** patients to any doctor and watch their queue and wait times expand.\n2. **Consultation Delays:** Inject a **+15m** or **+30m** delay and watch subsequent patient expected times update.\n3. **Doctor Breaks:** Put a doctor on break and watch their status toggle to \"On Break\" and queues adjust.\n4. **Bed Admissions & Discharges:** Simulate ward entries/exits to see hourly forecast curves move.\n5. **Epidemiological Outbreaks:** Trigger a **+15 Dengue Surge** to demonstrate the linked inventory alarm.\n6. **One-Click Baseline Reset:** Instantly resets the database to the clean demonstration baseline.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Machine Learning Evaluation (Section 16)\n\nCalculated on an 80/20 train/test split:\n\n| Metric | Random Forest Regressor (Tuned) | Multiple Linear Regression (Baseline) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **R-Squared ($R^2$)** | **0.9962 (99.6%)** | **0.8606 (86.1%)** |\n| **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)** | **10.00 minutes** | **59.69 minutes** |\n| **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)** | **13.73 minutes** | **82.89 minutes** |\n\n### Top Feature Importances (Random Forest):\n1. **`patients_ahead`** (41.8%): Number of active patients queued ahead\n2. **`avg_consult_duration`** (27.6%): Physician consultation velocity\n3. **`priority_code`** (14.2%): Triage classification (Emergency vs Normal)\n4. **`doctor_workload`** (8.4%): Total active department backlog\n5. **`doctor_available`** (4.1%): Active clinic presence vs interruptions\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification & Automated Test Results\n\nAn automated integration test suite (`test_app.py`) was executed:\n```\n..........\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 10 tests in 3.424s\n\nOK\n[TEST 01 PASS] Index HTML served\n[TEST 02 PASS] Overview KPIs loaded: 82 patients, 64 waiting\n[TEST 03 PASS] Patient registered: Token #183, Predicted wait: 37 mins\n[TEST 04 PASS] Dynamic delay cascade verified\n[TEST 05 PASS] Full patient consultation lifecycle verified (WAITING -> CALLED -> IN_CONSULTATION -> COMPLETED)\n[TEST 06 PASS] Workload Reassignment Approved: 6 patients redistributed\n[TEST 07 PASS] Bed occupancy & 4-hour horizon verified\n[TEST 08 PASS] Disease surveillance and surge simulation verified\n[TEST 09 PASS] Inventory risk analysis and restock verified\n[TEST 10 PASS] ML Metrics verified: Random Forest R² = 0.9962, MAE = 10.0 mins\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. How to Present This at the Hackathon (3rd-Semester Guide)\n\n1. **The Hook (1 minute):**\n   > *\"Existing hospital OPD systems only tell a patient who is currently inside the clinic. They don't predict how long you will wait, whether a doctor is overloaded, or if the hospital will run out of beds in three hours. Queue Oracle transforms hospital management from reactive to predictive.\"*\n\n2. **The Dynamic Queue Demo (2 minutes):**\n   - Go to **Patient Queue** (`Section 3`). Point out **Dr. Rajesh Sharma** with an active patient.\n   - Click **Record Delay** and enter `25` minutes. Show the judges how all subsequent patient consultation clocks automatically shift forward by 25 minutes.\n   - Go to **Wait Time Prediction** (`Section 4`), type token `101`, and show the explainable input weights.\n\n3. **The Intelligent Rebalancing Demo (1 minute):**\n   - Go to **Reassignment Recommendations** (`Section 10`).\n   - Explain: *\"Dr. Rajesh Sharma has 16 patients waiting, while Dr. Priya Nair has only 3. The system calculates that transferring 6 patients saves 55 minutes of queue time.\"*\n   - Click **APPROVE REASSIGNMENT** and show the judges that both queues rebalance instantly.\n\n4. **Resource & Disease Forecasting (1.5 minutes):**\n   - Go to **Bed Occupancy** (`Section 7`): Show the **+1h, +2h, +3h, +4h** forecast chart and the Cardiology bed shortage warning.\n   - Go to **Disease Trends** (`Section 8`): Show the 14-day Dengue surge and explain how it automatically flags low stock in **Dengue NS1 Antigen Kits** under **Inventory** (`Section 9`).\n\n5. **ML Performance (`Section 13`):**\n   - Show the live comparison table: *\"We trained a Random Forest model with 1,500 historical records. It achieves an $R^2$ of 0.996 and an MAE of 10 minutes, significantly outperforming linear regression because triage priority and doctor fatigue are non-linear relationships.\"*","thinking":"Completed the assigned task. The PowerShell command initiates a new process, which may introduce a binding delay, especially in sandboxed environments. Unit tests, executed successfully, cover all routes using a test client.\n\n\nAll core functionalities and project requirements are verified as implemented and returning expected results. Comprehensive testing confirms the system addresses OPD tokens, queue imbalances, bed shortages, and disease surge scenarios. The technology stack aligns with the project specifications, and the administrator dashboard provides the required data points.\n\n\nThe system now predicts patient waiting times, incorporating factors like doctor availability, patient priority, and department load. Dynamic recalculation addresses consultation delays, updating expected times for all queued patients under the same doctor, and incorporating early completions. Patient consultation status tracking provides a workflow for moving through WAITING, CALLED, IN_CONSULTATION, and COMPLETED stages, updating the active queue and recording actual consultation duration when complete.\n\n\nThe system now integrates doctor availability updates, recalculating patient wait times and refreshing department workload data on the dashboard. Developed a disease trend analysis module predicting future cases of key illnesses by analyzing trends. Implemented intelligent doctor workload balancing, recommending patient reassignments to address imbalances and estimate wait time reductions, subject to approval. The system now predicts department-wise bed occupancy, forecasting potential shortages and suggesting administrative actions. Inventory and medicine preparedness is connected to the disease trend analysis.\n\n\nDeveloped an IV fluid alert system; also predicting staffing needs using department demand and patient-to-doctor ratios, pinpointing strained areas. An administrator decision support system now gives recommendations with impact analysis and prioritized actions, including approval workflows and histories.\n\nAn interactive simulation lab allows evaluating diverse scenarios by manipulating patient arrivals, priority, and resource changes. Integrated data visualizations present key metrics using nine charts.\n\n\n"}
